Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 15:18:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] moe cache: LLAMA_MOE_PCIE_FRAC, a share of a step's missed
 experts computed on the GPU

Strata code in this patch: Copyright (c) 2026 Niko1221 and the Strata contributors, MIT License
(LICENSE added in Strata c0107c3, blob 6687a423dde6b7aff1b748c07f5ee93ff788089c; the full notice is
engines/patches/llama-upstream-flash/LICENSE.strata). Every ported function names its Strata file and commit:
the split rule and plan are src/core/expert_source.cpp @ d551edf :282-392 (`expert_pool_dispatch_multi`; the
same rule at d9ab843 :1666 and :1683); the bandwidth probe and the share rule are src/program/generate.cpp @
d9ab843 :950 and :1715-1735 (`probe_pcie_h2d_gbps`, the 20 / 4 GB/s rule; NOT in d551edf, which has only a fixed
per-pack default).

Off unless LLAMA_MOE_PCIE_FRAC is set; with it unset nothing in llama-moecache-pcie.* runs and the graph is the
one 0003/0004/0007/0013 build (the cache chain's activation is one lambda now, the same nodes in the same order).

LLAMA_MOE_PCIE_FRAC=<f> (0 < f <= 1) or "auto". Per layer and step, of the DISTINCT experts the router picked
that the VRAM cache does not hold ("missed"), the last m = (missed * pcie_num) >> 8 in routing order (Strata's
rule, pcie_num = round(f * 256), at most the pool's slots) are computed on the GPU, their weights copied over
PCIe from the pinned host experts (0005), while the CPU computes the rest. "auto" probes the host->device
bandwidth once at load (eight 64 MiB pinned copies timed around a stream synchronise) and applies Strata's rule,
min(base, max(0.05, base * GB/s / 26)), none below 4 GB/s and base from 20 GB/s up; LLAMA_MOE_PCIE_BASE
(default 0.55, Strata's value on their CPU kernels) is the base. The probe is a starting point for a sweep of
explicit shares, not a result: the base was measured on Strata's CPU kernels, not on 0008's.

How, with no new kernel and no new sched split (src/llama-moecache-pcie.{h,cpp}, src/llama-graph.cpp):
- the GPU side is a POOL of expert slots per device and expert shape (up, gate, down, [.., slots + 1], the last
  slot zero) and the same mul_mat_id + activation chain the cache already runs, over the pool's slot ids; a pool
  slot holds one whole expert. One pool serves every layer: layers run one after another, and the CPU split's
  input copy has synchronised the compute stream before a layer's plan runs, so a slot is never overwritten
  while a previous layer's chain still reads it. VRAM: up to (n_expert_used * batch limit * f) + 1 slots of
  1.38 MiB (Flash-Next, f = 0.29: 21 slots, ~29 MiB), logged at load; if it does not fit the feature turns itself off.
- a PLAN node (a ggml custom op, on the CPU backend because its table input is host memory) runs first in the
  layer's CPU split: it reads the routing ids and the cache's host table, writes the table the three CPU
  mul_mat_id nodes skip by (the cache's, with the pool's experts added) and the pool's slot ids, and enqueues the
  share's copies on a second CUDA stream (a second backend instance and an event: backend_copier), so they run
  while the CPU computes.
- a WAIT node after the CPU chain blocks on that event and passes the slot ids on; only the pool's GPU chain reads
  them, so it cannot be launched before the copies have landed. The graph is built in the order CPU chain, wait,
  cache chain, pool chain; the split count per layer is unchanged.
- the pool is the reason this is not two MUL_MAT_ID nodes with one op-offloaded (docs/FLASH-NEXT.md 10.2's plan):
  an offloaded node cannot skip ids, so the ids of the experts it does not own would still be computed (garbage
  from slots nothing copied, times a zero weight is NaN) or copy a dummy expert on every layer where the share is
  empty, and ggml's used-expert copy inside the scheduler is synchronous, so nothing would overlap.

Tests (tests/test-moe-pcie-split.cpp, registered with ctest, --quick; a CPU backend run needs no GPU and hides
every CUDA device itself): the share rule and the env parse; 5,000 random partitions against a brute force (every
position served by exactly one of cache / pool / CPU, the share is the last m missed experts in routing order,
the CPU table is the cache table plus the share, strided ids); a graph at the model's expert shape (iq2_s 2560 ->
640 gate/up, q2_0 640 -> 2560 down, top-10, 1-7 tokens, duplicates across tokens, random residency, the share at
0 / 8 / 74 / 141 / 256 of 256) compared two ways: with the unsplit mul_mat_id chain (the tolerance 1e-5 on the CPU
backend, 2e-2 with --device, where the GPU's kernels run on q8_1 activations), and EXACTLY (1e-6) with the same
cache chain over a cache that also holds the pool's experts, which runs the same kernels on the same rows in one
chain; the pool is poisoned (0xFF, NaN) before every step and the copies land only when the wait node runs; and a
control that drops the wait and must fail. CPU backend, 64 experts, 60 trials: 25,687 checks, 0 failed, every
difference 0 (bit-exact, against both references), the pool took 371 experts over 37 steps. With --device NAME (the caller restricts CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES; run on an RTX A4000, 60 trials, 64 experts: 25,567 checks, 0
failed, exact against the enlarged cache, within 1.6% of the CPU reference, the pool took the same 371 experts) the cache
and pool live on that device, the experts in its pinned host buffer, the copies go through the real second stream and
event, and the graph is scheduled over {device, CPU} as the engine does. The graph's inputs go through GPU nodes first
(x scaled, the ids as a strided view of a contiguous copy, the MoE output scaled): in the engine the router and the
next layer's work are on the GPU and that is what places the cache and pool chains there, whereas a graph of CPU inputs
and device weights ran entirely on the CPU, with the device pool copied to the host at the start of the split -- before
the plan had enqueued its copies -- which read the poison (the first device run's NaN was that harness, not the engine).
The control holds the copies back (a backlog on the second stream cannot make them late: on this card the compute
stream waited for it too) and drops the wait; it is detected in every run. Also checked in the engine (llama-server,
a tiny generated olmoe with Q4_0 experts, `--n-cpu-moe 99 --moe-expert-cache 1`, LLAMA_MOE_PCIE_FRAC=1.0 and 0.5,
poison on): the plan and wait nodes are placed on the CPU, every missed expert goes through the pool, and the next-token
logprobs agree with the GPU cache path to 3.5e-3, the same distance the cache path has from a full-GPU run. With the
switch unset the graph (GGML_SCHED_DEBUG=2: 94 splits, 1,360 nodes, the cache chain included) is identical to the one
the 0001-0022 build and the int0028 build print. Nothing is measured for speed: whether the GPU's share is faster than
the CPU's own experts at 0008's speed is the gate's question (docs/FLASH-NEXT.md 10.2).
