+// Yamadori 0024: pcie_frac -- the split of one layer's missed experts between the CPU and a GPU pool, against the unsplit
+// mul_mat_id (src/llama-moecache-pcie.h). The graph below is built with the same calls, in the same order, as
+// llm_graph_context::build_moe_ffn (src/llama-graph.cpp) does for a cached layer, and the plan / wait nodes and the
+// pool chain are the functions that file calls.
+//
+//   (a) off: the cache alone (the CPU skips the resident experts, the cache chain serves them) equals the unsplit chain
+//       -- this is the graph the engine builds without LLAMA_MOE_PCIE_FRAC;
+//   (b) on: CPU + cache chain + pool chain equal the unsplit chain, at the model's expert shape (iq2_s gate/up
+//       2560 -> 640, q2_0 down 640 -> 2560, top-10) on random routing with duplicates across tokens, random
+//       residency, 1..7 tokens, ids as a plain tensor and as a strided view;
+//   (c) every pool slot is poisoned (NaN) before each step and the copies land only when the wait node runs: a run in
+//       which the wait is dropped must FAIL (the test proves it can see a slot read before its copy completed), and
+//       the real runs must not read one.
+//
+//   test-moe-pcie-split [--quick] [--n-expert N] [--device NAME] [--trials N] [--seed S]
+//
+// With --device (a GPU device name, e.g. CUDA0) the cache and the pool live on that device, the weights in its pinned
+// host buffer, the copies go through a second backend instance and an event (moe_pcie::backend_copier), and the graph
+// is scheduled over {device, CPU} as the engine does; without it everything is on the CPU backend and the copies are a
+// fake that lands them only when wait() is called.
+
+#include "../src/llama-moecache-pcie.h"
+
+#include "ggml.h"
+#include "ggml-alloc.h"
